1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,243
An engineer on your team is in
the middle of a conflicted merge.

2
00:00:08,283 --> 00:00:13,090
It's going badly, so they run git merge
--abort. The manual says that this returns

3
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:16,991
you to the state before the merge.
The merge is gone, as promised.

4
00:00:17,031 --> 00:00:21,838
So is an edit to a prompt file that they
made an hour before the merge started and

5
00:00:21,878 --> 00:00:22,783
never committed.

6
00:00:23,123 --> 00:00:28,122
Nobody typed a wrong command. The safety
net has a hole, and the hole has an exact

7
00:00:28,162 --> 00:00:33,100
shape. By the end of this video you can
say what that shape is, and you can leave

8
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:37,463
any stopped merge in a state you can
name. Keep that lost edit in mind.

9
00:00:37,503 --> 00:00:38,324
It comes back.

10
00:00:40,164 --> 00:00:44,038
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:44,078 --> 00:00:49,300
A merge joins another branch's history into
yours, and a conflict is a place where

12
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:54,563
Git can't decide what a file should say.
In the previous video you took a conflict

13
00:00:54,603 --> 00:00:59,183
apart. The marker file in the working
tree, which is your files on disk.

14
00:00:59,223 --> 00:01:03,804
Stages 1, 2 and 3 in the index, which
is Git's draft of the next commit.

15
00:01:03,844 --> 00:01:09,066
And the MERGE_* files in the dot git folder.
This video is about what you do next.

16
00:01:09,406 --> 00:01:14,495
There are four parts. First, the checks
Git makes before a merge is allowed to

17
00:01:14,535 --> 00:01:19,952
start, because half of the questions people
ask about merges are about a merge that

18
00:01:19,992 --> 00:01:24,555
never began. Second, the five steps
from "conflict" to "merge commit".

19
00:01:24,595 --> 00:01:30,012
Third, the whole-file shortcuts, --ours and
--theirs, and what they discard without

20
00:01:30,052 --> 00:01:35,470
telling you. Fourth, the three exits:
--continue, --abort and --quit, and what each

21
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:36,653
one leaves behind.

22
00:01:36,993 --> 00:01:41,311
The project is still evalkit, the
evaluation harness for LLM outputs.

23
00:01:41,351 --> 00:01:46,489
You are on main. A branch, remember, is a
name that points at one commit and moves

24
00:01:46,529 --> 00:01:50,594
forward when you commit. Asha's
branch is feature/creative-judge.

25
00:01:50,634 --> 00:01:54,762
Everything on screen is Git. GitHub
does not appear in this video.

26
00:01:56,602 --> 00:01:58,180
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:01:58,520 --> 00:01:59,932
After this video you can:

28
00:02:00,272 --> 00:02:02,859
Run the checks that tell
whether a merge can start.

29
00:02:03,199 --> 00:02:06,662
Resolve a conflict path by
path and conclude the merge.

30
00:02:07,002 --> 00:02:10,506
Take one side of a path and
explain what else that discards.

31
00:02:10,846 --> 00:02:17,560
Choose between --abort, --continue and
--quit, and state what each leaves behind.

32
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:21,363
Bring back the conflicted state of
a path that was resolved wrongly.

33
00:02:23,203 --> 00:02:28,001
Start with why a workflow is needed at all.
A stopped merge is not an error state.

34
00:02:28,041 --> 00:02:32,721
It's a merge that Git has done as far as
the three-way rule allows, and it's now

35
00:02:32,761 --> 00:02:35,730
waiting for a decision that
only a person can make.

36
00:02:35,770 --> 00:02:40,450
Git has saved everything it knows: the
three versions of each conflicted path as

37
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:43,754
index stages, and the commit
being merged in MERGE_HEAD.

38
00:02:44,094 --> 00:02:48,131
A commit, remember, is one saved
snapshot of the whole project.

39
00:02:48,171 --> 00:02:53,243
Your job is to turn each unmerged path
into one stage 0 entry and then record a

40
00:02:53,283 --> 00:02:54,797
commit with two parents.

41
00:02:55,137 --> 00:02:57,508
The textbook gives that job five steps.

42
00:02:57,848 --> 00:03:02,817
Step 1, see what is open. git
status lists the unmerged paths.

43
00:03:02,857 --> 00:03:07,180
git diff --name-only
--diff-filter=U lists only those.

44
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:12,768
Step 2, learn both intents. The textbook's
sentence is worth repeating: resolving a

45
00:03:12,808 --> 00:03:16,017
conflict is an investigation,
not an editing chore.

46
00:03:16,057 --> 00:03:19,712
The stage diffs from the last
video tell you what changed.

47
00:03:19,752 --> 00:03:24,872
git log --merge tells you why, because it
lists the commits from either side that

48
00:03:24,912 --> 00:03:29,459
touch a conflicted path. If the intent
is still unclear, ask the author.

49
00:03:29,799 --> 00:03:35,257
Step 3, write the result. Edit the file
until it says what the merged project should

50
00:03:35,297 --> 00:03:40,493
say, and delete the marker lines. The
answer may be one side, both sides, or new

51
00:03:40,533 --> 00:03:40,820
text.

52
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:46,647
Step 4, mark it resolved. git add on an
unmerged path replaces stages 1 to 3 with

53
00:03:46,687 --> 00:03:51,151
one stage 0 entry. That's all it
does. It doesn't check your work.

54
00:03:51,191 --> 00:03:54,700
It stages whatever is in
the file, markers included.

55
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:00,696
Step 5, conclude. git merge --continue
checks that a merge is in progress and then

56
00:04:00,736 --> 00:04:04,308
runs git commit. Plain git
commit does the same job.

57
00:04:04,648 --> 00:04:09,842
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper:
write the five steps from memory, one verb

58
00:04:09,882 --> 00:04:11,739
each. Then say them out loud.

59
00:04:16,579 --> 00:04:20,073
Here they are: see, learn,
write, mark, conclude.

60
00:04:20,413 --> 00:04:26,010
Now the internals, because the exits
only make sense from the inside.

61
00:04:26,050 --> 00:04:32,709
Here are today's commits. main has 5397d5f,
Asha's branch has 640bfe1 and 45a7a67,

62
00:04:32,749 --> 00:04:34,915
and both grow from 6ae3c51.

63
00:04:35,255 --> 00:04:40,769
While a merge is open, .git holds MERGE_HEAD,
MERGE_MODE, MERGE_MSG and AUTO_MERGE.

64
00:04:40,809 --> 00:04:46,188
MERGE_HEAD names the commit being merged,
and it's the reason the commit you make

65
00:04:46,228 --> 00:04:51,809
will have a second parent. Remove that file
and the same files, staged the same way,

66
00:04:51,849 --> 00:04:55,756
produce a commit with one parent.
Hold on to that sentence.

67
00:04:55,796 --> 00:04:57,028
It explains --quit.

68
00:04:57,368 --> 00:05:01,114
When does the workflow not apply?
When the merge never started.

69
00:05:01,154 --> 00:05:06,102
Git protects uncommitted work by refusing
to begin, and the manual gives two rules.

70
00:05:06,142 --> 00:05:11,029
Git stops when local changes overlap with
files that the merge may need to update.

71
00:05:11,069 --> 00:05:16,017
And it aborts if there are any changes
registered in the index relative to the HEAD

72
00:05:16,057 --> 00:05:21,065
commit. HEAD is Git's name for where you
are now. So under the second rule, a staged

73
00:05:21,105 --> 00:05:24,851
change blocks the merge even in
a file the merge doesn't touch.

74
00:05:25,191 --> 00:05:30,451
And the failure mode this video is named
for: git merge --abort is defined by the

75
00:05:30,491 --> 00:05:33,853
manual as git reset --merge
while MERGE_HEAD exists.

76
00:05:33,893 --> 00:05:39,349
It resets every staged path. It can't tell
a merge result from an edit of yours that

77
00:05:39,389 --> 00:05:42,948
predates the merge and that
you staged in the meantime.

78
00:05:44,788 --> 00:05:49,938
A picture helps. Think of a stopped merge
as a form that Git has filled in as far as

79
00:05:49,978 --> 00:05:55,128
it could and handed back to you. The fields
it couldn't fill are the unmerged paths.

80
00:05:55,168 --> 00:05:59,330
git add is you signing one field.
--continue is handing the form in.

81
00:05:59,370 --> 00:06:03,347
--abort is tearing the form up and
going back to where you stood.

82
00:06:03,387 --> 00:06:08,413
--quit is throwing away the cover sheet
that says "this is a merge", while leaving

83
00:06:08,453 --> 00:06:10,700
every half-filled field on your desk.

84
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:15,794
Where the picture breaks: tearing up a paper
form can't damage anything that was on

85
00:06:15,834 --> 00:06:20,473
your desk before. --abort can. It resets
the index and the working tree for every

86
00:06:20,513 --> 00:06:25,209
staged path, and an older edit of yours
that you staged during the merge looks, to

87
00:06:25,249 --> 00:06:27,404
Git, exactly like a field of the form.

88
00:06:27,744 --> 00:06:33,035
The second model is for the shortcuts.
--ours and --theirs do not mean "our side of

89
00:06:33,075 --> 00:06:37,852
the conflict". They mean "the whole of
stage 2" and "the whole of stage 3".

90
00:06:37,892 --> 00:06:42,541
A file is the unit, not a hunk, and a
hunk is one block of changed lines.

91
00:06:44,381 --> 00:06:49,119
Quick quiz, from the picture.
Which exit moves the branch?

92
00:06:49,159 --> 00:06:52,744
A, continue. B, abort.
C, quit. Your answer?

93
00:06:57,584 --> 00:07:01,781
Read the bottom row. It's A: only
--continue moves the branch.

94
00:07:01,821 --> 00:07:07,452
--abort and --quit both delete the merge
state, and they differ in everything else.

95
00:07:07,492 --> 00:07:12,782
One cleans the index and the files. The
other leaves them exactly as they are.

96
00:07:14,622 --> 00:07:20,165
Part 1: before the merge starts. Into
the lab. You are on main in evalkit.

97
00:07:20,205 --> 00:07:24,919
You append one comment line to
evalkit/metrics.py and stage it.

98
00:07:24,959 --> 00:07:27,787
Asha's branch doesn't touch that file.

99
00:07:28,127 --> 00:07:32,427
Predict: the merge does not
touch metrics.py. Does it start?

100
00:07:32,467 --> 00:07:34,380
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

101
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:44,221
It does not. If you said yes, you're in
good company: the message points the wrong

102
00:07:44,261 --> 00:07:47,418
way. Look at the exit status,
2, and at the message.

103
00:07:47,458 --> 00:07:52,521
It names the file and says it would be
overwritten, which is not true of this file.

104
00:07:52,561 --> 00:07:57,378
The real reason is the manual's second
rule: any change registered in the index

105
00:07:57,418 --> 00:08:01,128
relative to HEAD stops the merge.
The message doesn't say so.

106
00:08:01,468 --> 00:08:06,001
An untracked file that the merge would
create is protected in the same way.

107
00:08:06,041 --> 00:08:09,110
Untracked means the file
has no entry in the index.

108
00:08:09,150 --> 00:08:13,195
The branch adds NOTES.md, and you
have your own untracked NOTES.md.

109
00:08:13,535 --> 00:08:17,270
One more refusal, which is not
about your working tree at all.

110
00:08:17,310 --> 00:08:20,801
The merge base is the best
common ancestor of two commits.

111
00:08:20,841 --> 00:08:24,149
Two commits with no common
ancestor have no merge base.

112
00:08:24,489 --> 00:08:28,731
git merge-base prints nothing and exits
with 1, and the merge is refused.

113
00:08:29,071 --> 00:08:34,754
Part 2: during the merge. Now a merge that
does start and stops on the conflict in

114
00:08:34,794 --> 00:08:40,477
config/eval.yaml. Suppose you edit nothing
and run git add on the conflicted file.

115
00:08:40,517 --> 00:08:44,804
Predict what git status --short
shows for it. Say it out loud.

116
00:08:49,644 --> 00:08:55,108
M, staged, as if resolved. git add 🟢 is
labelled SAFE because it only collapses

117
00:08:55,148 --> 00:09:00,472
stages into stage 0 and adds a blob,
which is Git's stored copy of one file's

118
00:09:00,512 --> 00:09:06,324
content. But it accepted a file full of
markers. The last command is your guard: git

119
00:09:06,364 --> 00:09:12,037
diff --cached --check reports the leftover
conflict markers by line and exits with

120
00:09:12,077 --> 00:09:14,963
2. Run it before you conclude, every time.

121
00:09:15,303 --> 00:09:19,114
While a merge is open, a second merge
and a partial commit are both refused.

122
00:09:19,454 --> 00:09:23,966
Part 3: the whole-file shortcuts.
labs/run ch08/restore-sides.

123
00:09:24,006 --> 00:09:29,398
The merge is stopped again. Look at
the first grep: the conflict is on the

124
00:09:29,438 --> 00:09:35,345
temperature line, and line 11 says seed:
1234. That seed is Asha's change, in the

125
00:09:35,385 --> 00:09:37,841
same file, and it didn't conflict.

126
00:09:38,181 --> 00:09:43,201
git restore --ours 🔴 is labelled
DANGEROUS, so the five answers come first.

127
00:09:43,241 --> 00:09:48,869
What it changes: the working tree file only.
What it can destroy: your hand edits to

128
00:09:48,909 --> 00:09:52,850
that file, which exist nowhere
else until you git add them.

129
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:56,427
How to preview: git diff
--ours or git diff --theirs.

130
00:09:56,467 --> 00:10:02,027
How to recover: git restore --merge on the
path brings back the starting point, not

131
00:10:02,067 --> 00:10:07,155
your edits. When it is appropriate: when
the whole file from one side is the

132
00:10:07,195 --> 00:10:10,192
decision, or to restart
a botched resolution.

133
00:10:10,532 --> 00:10:13,632
Predict the seed after this
command. Say it out loud.

134
00:10:18,472 --> 00:10:23,551
The seed is 7 again. Asha's non-conflicting
change is gone from the working tree,

135
00:10:23,591 --> 00:10:26,900
and if you stage this file,
it's gone from the merge.

136
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:31,766
Also look at the last status: the path
is still UU, because the index was not

137
00:10:31,806 --> 00:10:32,272
touched.

138
00:10:32,612 --> 00:10:34,659
--theirs is the mirror image.

139
00:10:34,999 --> 00:10:39,664
And git restore --merge 🔴 rebuilds
the marker version from the stages.

140
00:10:39,704 --> 00:10:43,428
It's the undo for both shortcuts
and for a botched edit.

141
00:10:43,768 --> 00:10:48,052
Notice the marker labels: ours and
theirs, not HEAD and the branch name.

142
00:10:48,092 --> 00:10:51,836
The file was rebuilt from the
index, not by the original merge.

143
00:10:52,176 --> 00:10:55,664
Older scripts use git checkout
for the same three operations.

144
00:10:55,704 --> 00:10:58,671
You will read this form.
You don't need to write it.

145
00:10:59,011 --> 00:11:04,764
--merge also works after git add. Take their
whole file, stage it, then change your

146
00:11:04,804 --> 00:11:05,113
mind.

147
00:11:05,453 --> 00:11:10,974
After the git add there's one stage 0
entry. After git restore --merge there are

148
00:11:11,014 --> 00:11:16,744
three entries again, stages 1, 2 and 3. The
index keeps a record of the stages that

149
00:11:16,784 --> 00:11:18,412
the resolution replaced.

150
00:11:18,752 --> 00:11:24,152
Part 4: the three exits. labs/run
ch08/abort-continue-quit.

151
00:11:24,492 --> 00:11:29,725
git merge --continue 🟡 CAUTION: it creates
the merge commit and moves the branch.

152
00:11:29,765 --> 00:11:31,808
Run it too early and it refuses.

153
00:11:32,148 --> 00:11:37,609
git merge --abort 🔴 DANGEROUS. The five
answers. It changes the index, the working

154
00:11:37,649 --> 00:11:42,640
tree and the merge state. It destroys
every resolution made so far, and any

155
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:48,141
uncommitted edit that was staged during
the merge. Preview with git status and git

156
00:11:48,181 --> 00:11:53,776
diff --cached --stat. Recovery: staged
content survives as unreferenced objects that

157
00:11:53,816 --> 00:11:57,466
git fsck lists, and unstaged
resolution edits are gone.

158
00:11:57,506 --> 00:12:02,228
Appropriate when the merge was a
mistake or needs a different approach.

159
00:12:02,568 --> 00:12:05,995
The reflog is Git's local list
of the values HEAD has had.

160
00:12:06,035 --> 00:12:09,461
Predict what it will say after
the abort. Say it out loud.

161
00:12:14,301 --> 00:12:19,488
reset: moving to HEAD. The abort is
a reset, and the reflog says so.

162
00:12:19,528 --> 00:12:23,178
The MERGE_* files are
gone, and ORIG_HEAD stays.

163
00:12:23,518 --> 00:12:27,102
Outside a merge, both commands tell
you that MERGE_HEAD is missing.

164
00:12:27,442 --> 00:12:32,137
Now the odd one. git merge --quit 🟡
CAUTION deletes the merge state only.

165
00:12:32,477 --> 00:12:37,314
No MERGE_* file is left, and git status
no longer says "you are still merging".

166
00:12:37,354 --> 00:12:41,883
But the unmerged path is still there
and the staged change is still there.

167
00:12:41,923 --> 00:12:46,822
Predict: you resolve, add and commit now.
How many parents does the commit have?

168
00:12:46,862 --> 00:12:48,489
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

169
00:12:53,329 --> 00:12:59,963
One. No MERGE_HEAD, so no second parent.
The graph shows cdacf6e on top of 5397d5f

170
00:13:00,003 --> 00:13:03,138
and Asha's two commits off to the side.

171
00:13:03,478 --> 00:13:08,919
Here it is as a picture. MERGE_HEAD is
gone, and cdacf6e has one line into it.

172
00:13:08,959 --> 00:13:14,470
No line joins Asha's commits to it. Her
content is in main, her commits are not

173
00:13:14,510 --> 00:13:19,178
ancestors of it, and git branch
--no-merged still lists her branch.

174
00:13:19,218 --> 00:13:25,080
The way back after --quit is git reset
--merge, because --abort has nothing to find.

175
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:30,724
Part 5: uncommitted work and --autostash.
Work in paths the merge does not touch is

176
00:13:30,764 --> 00:13:33,879
allowed and survives both
the merge and an abort.

177
00:13:34,219 --> 00:13:39,439
An unstaged edit in a file the merge does
touch stops the merge, unless you let Git

178
00:13:39,479 --> 00:13:44,446
stash it. To stash is to park uncommitted
work as a commit, to be applied again

179
00:13:44,486 --> 00:13:47,361
later. merge.autoStash
makes that the default.

180
00:13:50,701 --> 00:13:55,152
Look at MERGE_AUTOSTASH in the
listing, and at "Applied autostash."

181
00:13:55,192 --> 00:14:00,515
after the abort. Then read the hint Git
printed: "apply stashed changes with git

182
00:14:00,555 --> 00:14:04,604
stash pop". labs/run
ch08/autostash-continue tests that hint.

183
00:14:04,944 --> 00:14:08,775
The stash list is empty, and
git stash pop finds nothing.

184
00:14:08,815 --> 00:14:13,869
The autostash is a stash-shaped commit
that .git/MERGE_AUTOSTASH points to.

185
00:14:13,909 --> 00:14:16,314
It's not an entry in the stash list.

186
00:14:16,654 --> 00:14:21,948
git merge --continue applied it for you.
According to the textbook, the autostash

187
00:14:21,988 --> 00:14:27,414
reaches the stash list after git merge
--quit or git reset --merge, per the manual,

188
00:14:27,454 --> 00:14:31,431
or when applying it conflicts,
which was seen in the sandbox.

189
00:14:31,471 --> 00:14:34,394
Only then is git stash
pop the right command.

190
00:14:36,234 --> 00:14:37,882
Five mistakes to watch for.

191
00:14:38,222 --> 00:14:43,873
Staging a file that still contains markers.
Root cause: git add records whatever is

192
00:14:43,913 --> 00:14:48,947
in the file and performs no check; git
diff --cached --check is the check.

193
00:14:49,287 --> 00:14:53,168
Taking --theirs to "accept
their side of the conflict".

194
00:14:53,208 --> 00:14:59,084
Root cause: --ours and --theirs write the
whole of stage 2 or stage 3, so the other

195
00:14:59,124 --> 00:15:03,503
side's non-conflicting changes
in that file are discarded too.

196
00:15:03,843 --> 00:15:07,042
Using --quit to get out of a
merge and then committing.

197
00:15:07,082 --> 00:15:11,871
Root cause: without MERGE_HEAD the commit
has one parent, so the content is in but

198
00:15:11,911 --> 00:15:13,991
the branch still counts as unmerged.

199
00:15:14,331 --> 00:15:20,455
Running git add -A during a merge, then
aborting. Root cause: --abort is git reset

200
00:15:20,495 --> 00:15:26,771
--merge, which resets every staged path and
cannot tell a merge result from your own

201
00:15:26,811 --> 00:15:27,598
older edit.

202
00:15:27,938 --> 00:15:34,221
Running git stash pop because the hint said
so. Root cause: the autostash lives in

203
00:15:34,261 --> 00:15:40,545
.git/MERGE_AUTOSTASH, not in the stash
list, and --continue or --abort applies it.

204
00:15:42,385 --> 00:15:47,786
Now, out of the lab. Your evaluation team
has a release requirement: scores must be

205
00:15:47,826 --> 00:15:53,095
reproducible. Asha's branch raises the
judge temperature to 0.7 for diversity and

206
00:15:53,135 --> 00:15:57,880
changes the seed. main set the temperature
to 0.0. The merge stops on the

207
00:15:57,920 --> 00:15:58,994
temperature line.

208
00:15:59,334 --> 00:16:04,618
The engineer who resolves it reads git log
--merge, and sees the two commits that

209
00:16:04,658 --> 00:16:10,138
touched the line and their reasons. They
keep 0.0. They keep Asha's new seed and her

210
00:16:10,178 --> 00:16:14,607
extra prompt line. They run the
tests and git diff --cached --check.

211
00:16:14,647 --> 00:16:20,061
And they write two sentences in the merge
message: temperature stays at 0.0 because

212
00:16:20,101 --> 00:16:25,450
reproducible scores are a release requirement,
and the seed and the rationale line

213
00:16:25,490 --> 00:16:26,896
are taken as they are.

214
00:16:27,236 --> 00:16:31,666
That message matters more than it looks.
The textbook's point is that the merge

215
00:16:31,706 --> 00:16:35,684
commit message is the only place where
a resolution can explain itself.

216
00:16:35,724 --> 00:16:40,041
One sentence there answers the question
that somebody will ask in six months.

217
00:16:41,881 --> 00:16:47,971
Your turn. Do Lab 6.7, "Abort and retry
with zdiff3", in lab-manual/m06-merge.md.

218
00:16:48,011 --> 00:16:54,101
Type it by hand in the lab shell. Your
commit IDs will differ from the transcript

219
00:16:54,141 --> 00:16:56,675
where you create commits yourself.

220
00:16:57,015 --> 00:16:59,537
Before you start, write
down three predictions:

221
00:16:59,877 --> 00:17:04,585
After git merge --abort, which of your
uncommitted edits are still there: the one

222
00:17:04,625 --> 00:17:09,509
you never staged, the one you staged before
the merge, the one you staged during it?

223
00:17:09,849 --> 00:17:12,994
What will git reflog -1
show after the abort?

224
00:17:13,334 --> 00:17:17,997
If an edit is lost, where could a copy of
it still exist, and which command would

225
00:17:18,037 --> 00:17:18,462
list it?

226
00:17:18,802 --> 00:17:22,460
The lab reproduces the loss from
the hook and recovers the edit.

227
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:25,697
That's the lost prompt edit
from the start, found again.

228
00:17:25,737 --> 00:17:28,413
