230
00:17:35,052 --> 00:17:38,700
half done", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

231
00:17:40,540 --> 00:17:45,609
Q136: "git merge --abort is supposed to
return you to the state before the merge.

232
00:17:45,649 --> 00:17:50,530
An engineer ran it during a conflicted
merge and lost an uncommitted edit that

233
00:17:50,570 --> 00:17:55,828
predated the merge. How did the safety net
fail, can the edit be recovered, and what

234
00:17:55,868 --> 00:17:57,216
do you tell the team?"

235
00:17:59,556 --> 00:18:02,503
Answer it out loud before
you open the answers file.

236
00:18:02,543 --> 00:18:06,064
A strong answer has three parts,
in the order of the question.

237
00:18:06,104 --> 00:18:10,602
It names the mechanism: what --abort is
defined as, and what that command can't

238
00:18:10,642 --> 00:18:15,312
distinguish. It gives a recovery path with
the conditions under which it works and

239
00:18:15,352 --> 00:18:19,793
the point at which it stops working,
without promising more than the mechanism

240
00:18:19,833 --> 00:18:24,446
gives. And it ends with a habit the team
can adopt tomorrow, stated as a rule and

241
00:18:24,486 --> 00:18:25,422
not as a warning.

242
00:18:27,262 --> 00:18:31,779
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say these sentences without notes.

243
00:18:32,119 --> 00:18:37,493
A merge does not start if anything is
staged, or if an unstaged or untracked file

244
00:18:37,533 --> 00:18:42,773
overlaps with what the merge would write.
Resolution is five steps: see what is

245
00:18:42,813 --> 00:18:47,185
open, learn both intents, write
the result, git add, and conclude.

246
00:18:47,225 --> 00:18:52,398
--ours and --theirs take a whole file
from stage 2 or stage 3, and git restore

247
00:18:52,438 --> 00:18:56,476
--merge brings the conflicted
state back, even after git add.

248
00:18:56,816 --> 00:19:02,798
--continue records a two-parent commit,
--abort is git reset --merge and returns to

249
00:19:02,838 --> 00:19:07,949
HEAD, and --quit forgets the merge
while leaving the half-merged files.

250
00:19:07,989 --> 00:19:12,665
An abort can lose an older edit
that was staged during the merge.

251
00:19:14,505 --> 00:19:17,369
